## 1. Ñāṇananda: the five ideas that belong in your film

I've now examined the text of Concept and Reality in Early Buddhist Thought, first published in 1971 and revised in 2012, as well as his later Nibbāna: The Mind Stilled sermons.

There are five particularly useful ideas, and they fit together almost like consecutive scenes.

### A. Papañca: when a simple perception turns into an entire world

Papañca is often translated as conceptual proliferation. But Ñāṇananda isn't simply talking about thinking too much.

He is examining how perception and thought can proliferate into apparently objective structures that overwhelm the person who constructs them.

His reading centres on the Madhupiṇḍika Sutta (MN 18).

The cognitive sequence in MN 18

Sense contact

Feeling

Perception

Thought

Conceptual proliferation

Interpretations and conceptual distinctions become self-reinforcing

The sutta's explanatory sequence; not a literal neural-processing pipeline or a claim that every thought is problematic.

The startling feature of this account is that the proliferating concepts eventually come back to trouble the thinker. What began as an interpretive activity acquires the appearance of an independent reality.

Ñāṇananda uses the image of a magician who reconstructs a tiger, only to be devoured by it.&#x20;

