### B. The magician who sees his own trick

This is probably the most useful Ñāṇananda passage for your film.

In The Magic of the Mind, he describes a magic performance from two perspectives.

One spectator watches the show and is enchanted by its apparent impossibilities. Another, positioned where he can see how the tricks are accomplished, understands the preparations behind the illusion.

Both witness the same event. But the experience is radically different.

He returns to the parable in Nibbāna: The Mind Stilled, Sermons 26–27, as an illustration of ignorance and dependent arising.&#x20;

