## 2. Sanskrit aesthetics: Abhinavagupta gives you another way to explain the audience

This could be one of the most original sections of the essay.

Rather than presenting Indian philosophy as something entirely separate from your analysis of comedy, introduce the Nāṭyaśāstra as an early attempt to analyse the experience of performance.

### Rasa: the aesthetic flavour of an experience

Classical Sanskrit aesthetics identifies rasa as the aesthetic flavour relished by a receptive spectator.

The traditional eight rasas were later expanded to a commonly recognised nine, with śānta (tranquillity) gaining particular importance in later aesthetic theory.

Śṛṅgāra

Love / beauty

Hāsya

Humour / mirth

Karuṇa

Compassion / sorrow

Raudra

Anger / fury

Vīra

Courage / heroism

Bhayānaka

Fear / terror

Bībhatsa

Disgust

Adbhuta

Wonder

Śānta

Peace / tranquillity

Eight rasas in the classical list; śānta is the influential later ninth.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://ebooks.inflibnet.ac.in\&sz=32)

Comparative Indian Drama

+1

There is something particularly interesting about hāsya rasa.

The Nāṭyaśāstra distinguishes six forms of laughter, ranging from a restrained smile through increasingly pronounced forms of laughter. It also examines how theatrical speech, costume and action produce comic effects.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://ebooks.inflibnet.ac.in\&sz=32)

Comparative Indian Drama



You could cut from a solemn presentation of Sanskrit terminology to increasingly ridiculous clips illustrating each degree of laughter.

But Abhinavagupta's contribution is deeper than a classification system.

He considers how the spectator's prepared emotional dispositions are activated by the performance. Through sādhāraṇīkaraṇa, the particular circumstances of the performance can become generalised, allowing the audience to experience an emotion aesthetically rather than simply as a personal reaction to an actual event.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://ebooks.inflibnet.ac.in\&sz=32)

Indian Literary Criticism and Theory



This provides a very useful counterpoint to modern predictive processing.

Neuroscience asks how the listener detects, predicts and interprets the performance.

Abhinavagupta asks what kind of experience is relished by the spectator.

Those aren't identical questions.

### An especially interesting discovery: Abhinavagupta on incongruity

I also found a specialist study, [Abhinavagupta's Conception of Humor, Chapter 9](https://www.svabhinava.org/hasy-abh/Thesis-09/Thesis-9.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com).

It discusses how incongruity, inappropriate conduct and the imitation or distortion of other emotions can become conditions for hāsya.

One particularly interesting interpretation is that laughter can influence how spectators perceive the incongruity itself. Audiences don't merely detect independently existing absurdities; the presentation can teach them what to regard as absurd.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://www.svabhinava.org\&sz=32)

chapter 9



That opens a great doorway into the relationship between humour and social norms.

A comedian doesn't always just violate established expectations.

Sometimes a comedian changes what the audience considers normal, and suddenly the ordinary world becomes funny.

That's a different kind of artistic achievement.

An observational comedian can spend five minutes explaining an everyday situation and, when you encounter that situation next week, you can no longer see it the old way.

That is a meaningful change in perception.

### The philosophical twist: contrasting Trika and Buddhism

Abhinavagupta's nondual Śaiva philosophy and Ñāṇananda's Buddhism shouldn't be presented as saying the same thing.

Their views of consciousness and self differ substantially.

But putting them beside each other creates an illuminating tension:

- Abhinavagupta offers a powerful account of how artistic experience can transcend ordinary individualised concerns and reveal something about consciousness.
- Ñāṇananda investigates how consciousness, language and identification condition our experience of self and world, and how insight can free us from attachment to them.

You can leave that disagreement unresolved. In fact, it fits your interest in tension and resolution.

## 3. Bhartṛhari: the punchline as a flash of meaning

This may be the most unexpectedly useful Sanskrit connection.

Bhartṛhari developed a theory of language in the Vākyapadīya, involving sphoṭa.

A central idea is that the meaning of an utterance isn't simply the sum of separately understood words. Meaning can be grasped as a whole through an intuitive act of recognition called pratibhā.

The [Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy](https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/literal-nonliteral-india/?utm_source=chatgpt.com) and the [Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy](https://iep.utm.edu/bhartrihari/?utm_source=chatgpt.com) have useful discussions of this.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://plato.stanford.edu\&sz=32)

Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy

+1



Now consider what happens when a punchline lands.

You have been processing a sentence word by word. Then one word causes you to reinterpret the entire preceding sentence.

The meaning appears to reorganise in a single moment.

That isn't proof of Bhartṛhari's metaphysics, but it's an excellent demonstration of the phenomenological problem he was analysing.

You could introduce an intentionally ambiguous sentence, let the audience form one interpretation, and reveal a final word or image that forces another.

Then show the same sentence again.

The words haven't changed.

The listener's experience has.

Potential script line: "A punchline doesn't always give you a new piece of information. Sometimes it gives you an entirely new past."

Because now, looking back, the setup itself seems to have meant something different.

That is an especially elegant route from comedy into perception, language and time.

