Start with your musical phrase.

Play Happy Birthday to the penultimate note and stop.

Let the listener anticipate the completion.

Resolve it.

Then repeat the passage, this time creating an obviously unexpected ending.

Finally, play it normally after telling them to expect another twist.

You've demonstrated that the same sort of sensory event can carry different expectations depending on context.

One stronger version of your idea: before the third rendition, explicitly announce that something unexpected will happen. Play it completely normally. Then explain that you've changed the listener's expectation without changing the song.

### Discovery two: humour involves both cognition and affect

A 2013 review in Nature Reviews Neuroscience, The Neural Basis of Humour Processing, identifies neural systems implicated in detecting or resolving incongruity as well as systems associated with reward and salience.&#x20;

