That gives you a useful distinction:

Understanding why something is a joke is not necessarily the same as finding it funny.

A perfectly understandable punchline might produce no amusement.

And something funny can make us laugh before we could explain its structure.

This is also the right point to introduce benign violation theory, which argues that humour often depends on a violation being perceived as acceptable or nonthreatening in some relevant sense.&#x20;

